        <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h">Prove the pattern on familiar ground first.</h2>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>A novel interaction and an unfamiliar domain, tested together, tell you nothing — you can't separate "the wheel is confusing" from "ultrasound is confusing." So we split them.</p>
        <div class="phases">
          <article class="card" data-reveal>
            <h6>Phase 1 · de-risk the paradigm</h6>
            <h4>The same wheel, driving a digital camera.</h4>
            <p>We built the radial wheel to navigate a camera's settings — features people already understood — and A/B'd it against flyout, dropdown, and accordion menus on the same familiar tasks. Monitoring, surveys, direct observation. Testing on the familiar isolates the <em>interaction</em> from the domain: we were judging the paradigm, not the product.</p>
            <p class="result">✓ The wheel won. Unanimously.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card" data-reveal>
            <h6>Phase 2 · transfer to the real stakes</h6>
            <h4>Then the iViz, with real users — and non-users.</h4>
            <p>We put the wheel into the ultrasound context and sat it in front of existing SonoSite users <em>and</em> people who'd never touched one. If the pattern only worked for experts, we'd have learned that here.</p>
            <p class="result">✓ Both groups breezed through.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ TUNING ============ -->
      <section class="block container narrow">
        <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h" data-reveal>Tuning the physics. Why it had to be code.</h2>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>This is the part a static wireframe can't do. A wheel that turns too fast shoots past the tissue layer you're measuring; too slow, and the thumb fatigues. Friction and inertia under the pad have to be <em>felt</em> to be tuned. The hitbox has to be larger than the graphic so a gloved thumb that drifts doesn't lose the control. Press-and-hold needs a real threshold — long enough that a slip never triggers it, short enough that it never feels stuck.</p>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>None of that is decidable in a drawing. That's why the industrial designers needed someone who could build the gesture, hand it to a nurse on an iPad Mini, and watch what her thumb did.</p>
